<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Overview</h2>
<p>
    Dorks &amp; Dice is a long-term project through which I developed and applied skills in requirements discovery,
    systems design, leadership, research, documentation, technical operations, and software planning. I founded the group
    in September 2024 and ran its principal Dungeons &amp; Dragons campaign through April 2026 while also building the
    infrastructure and processes needed to support the group beyond individual sessions.
</p>
<p>
    The project is professionally relevant because it required sustained ownership under real constraints. I had to understand
    what participants were trying to accomplish, prepare systems flexible enough to support unpredictable decisions, manage
    long live sessions and interpersonal issues, research unfamiliar domains, maintain continuity across hundreds of moving
    parts, and operate technical services used by the group.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Requirements Discovery And Participant-Centered Design</h2>
<p>
    My approach was to treat player goals as requirements to understand rather than requests to reject because they fell
    outside a prepared script. For almost anything the players wanted to attempt, I worked with them to determine how it
    could function within the rules and setting, what constraints applied, and what consequences would follow.
</p>
<p>
    This required identifying the intent behind a request, separating the desired outcome from a proposed implementation,
    and designing a path that preserved both participant agency and system consistency. That same process maps directly to
    software requirements gathering, client discovery, and user-centered product design.
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Translated loosely defined ideas into concrete rules, resources, risks, and implementation steps</li>
    <li>Supported multiple valid approaches rather than forcing a single predetermined workflow</li>
    <li>Balanced user freedom with consistency, fairness, maintainability, and long-term consequences</li>
    <li>Observed actual use and adjusted systems when existing tools or rules no longer met the need</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Planning, Facilitation, And Decision-Making</h2>
<p>
    Fool's Gold sessions lasted up to 12 hours, including meal breaks, and I typically spent at least two dozen additional
    hours each week preparing. The work involved far more than writing narrative content. I prepared interconnected scenarios,
    locations, characters, factions, encounters, rules interactions, and contingencies while maintaining continuity with
    everything the players had already changed.
</p>
<p>
    During live sessions, I had to process new information quickly, make defensible decisions, communicate those decisions
    clearly, and adapt without losing the wider objectives of the campaign. I also managed scheduling, expectations, and
    substantial interpersonal conflict while keeping a long-running collaborative project functioning.
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Long-range planning combined with weekly execution</li>
    <li>Rapid decisions under incomplete and changing information</li>
    <li>Sustained facilitation and public speaking over extended sessions</li>
    <li>Conflict resolution, expectation-setting, and stakeholder alignment</li>
    <li>Prioritization across immediate needs, future dependencies, and limited preparation time</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Research And Rapid Domain Learning</h2>
<p>
    Supporting player-driven ideas regularly required me to learn unfamiliar subjects well enough to integrate them into a
    coherent system. When one player developed a paladin focused on blacksmithing and item invention, I studied basic
    blacksmithing and metallurgy so materials, repairs, crafting choices, and custom equipment could be handled with more
    depth and internal consistency.
</p>
<p>
    I applied the same research process across naval travel, vehicle systems, flight, interstellar travel, crafting, and
    complex encounter design. Because I maintain a zero-tolerance policy toward piracy, I also obtained lawful access to the
    fifth-edition source material needed to support those possibilities rather than relying on unauthorized copies.
</p>
<p>
    This work demonstrates my ability to identify a knowledge gap, locate and evaluate relevant material, learn the concepts
    needed for the immediate problem, and integrate them into an existing system without breaking established behavior.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Systems Integration And Complexity Management</h2>
<p>
    The campaign eventually crossed boundaries that were not part of its original scope. Interstellar travel, for example,
    was not planned at the beginning. By the time the players had developed the means to pursue it, I had prepared the rules,
    setting logic, and dependencies necessary to support it without discarding prior continuity.
</p>
<p>
    The Fool's Gold finale required several linked operations to run at once: part of the party fought Godric III directly,
    other players operated a mech dragon using Spelljammer mechanics, allied forces attacked a sun extractor, the army of a
    player-founded nation fought Godric's forces with rebel support, and Xanu controlled soul-powered mechanical dinosaurs.
    The encounter took place in another solar system reached through a city-sized portal powered by stored energy previously
    extracted from a god-star.
</p>
<p>
    The encounter remained challenging for hours even though the final attack dealt 1,372 damage. That was possible because
    success depended on several interacting objectives, states, resources, and consequences rather than one conventional
    health pool. Professionally, this is an example of designing and operating a complex system whose components remained
    individually meaningful while contributing to one larger outcome.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Process Improvement And Software Product Thinking</h2>
<p>
    Repeated use exposed limitations in conventional initiative trackers. Loot Tavern crafting gave players substantial
    control over their equipment, but the resulting power scaling made ordinary single-target encounters difficult to run.
    I adapted the kaiju mechanics from <em>Ryoko's Guide to the Yokai Realms</em> for bosses of any physical size, allowing
    encounters to use separate weak points, rage, active mechanics, and evolving states instead of relying on one health bar.
</p>
<p>
    The approach produced better encounters but increased the amount of information I had to track manually. That led to a
    clear software requirement: an initiative and encounter-management tool capable of tracking standard combat alongside
    weak-point health, rage, mechanics, and boss state.
</p>
<p>
    This is the first planned Dorks &amp; Dice web tool. It demonstrates a product-development process grounded in direct
    experience: identify a recurring workflow problem, understand why existing tools fail, define the required behavior,
    and build a reusable solution around actual use rather than hypothetical features.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Technical Operations</h2>
<p>
    Dorks &amp; Dice expanded beyond tabletop sessions into persistent services. I launched a Minecraft server in October 2025
    and a Hytale server in January 2026, supported by the group's Discord presence and website. Operating those services
    required practical work across hardware, Linux, containers, networking, remote access, deployment, troubleshooting,
    and ongoing maintenance.
</p>
<p>
    This gave the project a second professional dimension: I was not only designing experiences and processes, but also
    operating the technical environment on which the group depended. The systems existed for real users and required
    reliability, documentation, and continued ownership.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Professional Skills Demonstrated</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Requirements analysis:</strong> uncovering the actual goal behind open-ended or unconventional requests</li>
    <li><strong>User-centered design:</strong> creating flexible systems around participant needs instead of forcing rigid workflows</li>
    <li><strong>Systems thinking:</strong> managing dependencies and interactions across rules, people, technology, and long-term continuity</li>
    <li><strong>Project ownership:</strong> sustaining a multi-year effort from planning through operation and iteration</li>
    <li><strong>Facilitation and leadership:</strong> guiding extended collaborative sessions and maintaining alignment</li>
    <li><strong>Conflict resolution:</strong> addressing interpersonal problems without abandoning project goals or participant needs</li>
    <li><strong>Research:</strong> learning unfamiliar domains and applying them to concrete requirements</li>
    <li><strong>Decision-making:</strong> responding to changing conditions with limited time and incomplete information</li>
    <li><strong>Documentation:</strong> preserving complex systems, decisions, and continuity for later use</li>
    <li><strong>Technical operations:</strong> administering servers, networks, containers, deployments, and public services</li>
    <li><strong>Product thinking:</strong> converting repeated workflow problems into specific software requirements</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Current Direction</h2>
<p>
    The major tabletop campaigns are complete, while the Minecraft server, Hytale server, website, and Dorks &amp; Dice identity
    provide a foundation for future work. My immediate development focus is the initiative and complex encounter tracker,
    followed by additional Game Master tools derived from workflows I experienced directly.
</p>

